  /* ⚠ THE RING TURNS; THE LABELS DO NOT. The set rotates one way and every
     satellite counter-rotates the same amount over the same duration, so the
     constellation travels while each icon and word stays upright. Rotating the
     set alone would carry the labels round with it and read them out sideways
     and upside-down at the bottom of the circle.

     These are the INDIVIDUAL `rotate` properties, not `transform`. The satellite
     already uses `translate: -50% -50%` to sit on its point, and the individual
     properties compose in a fixed order — translate, then rotate — so the
     counter-turn happens about the satellite's own centre after it has been
     placed. Folding both into one `transform` would make the order my problem
     and re-open exactly the bug that once collapsed a constellation onto its
     centre.

     42s per revolution — quickened from 70s at the client's request. Still slow
     enough that the eye reads it as drift rather than as a spinning graphic. */
  @keyframes sc-orbit { to { rotate: 360deg; } }
  @keyframes sc-orbit-back { to { rotate: -360deg; } }

  .sc-eco__set { animation: sc-orbit 42s linear infinite; }
  .sat { animation: sc-orbit-back 42s linear infinite; }

  /* Placed by percentage — see the note on `orbit` for why this is not a
     rotate→translate transform. NO PLATE: on sand the ornament artwork reads
     unaided, which is what lets the satellites vary in size and weight. */
  /* ⚠ CENTRED WITH NEGATIVE MARGINS, NOT WITH A TRANSFORM — AND THAT IS THE
     WHOLE POINT. Two earlier attempts put the centring on the same element as
     the counter-rotation: first `translate: -50% -50%`, then a zero-size box
     with `place-items: center`. Both let the rotation swing the offset, so each
     satellite's centre orbited its own half-diagonal instead of holding station
     — "Ambition" was measured drifting 73px in and out of the core while short
     labels barely moved. A wobble, not an orbit.

     With a declared width and height and margins of exactly half each, the box's
     centre sits ON its point with no transform involved. The only transform left
     is the counter-turn, and rotating a box about its own centre cannot move
     that centre. The height is fixed so the margin can be half of something
     knowable; `place-content: center` then centres the icon and word inside it. */
  .sat {
